hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2803","**
Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, narrated to us Hammad (meaning Ibn Zayd), from Ayyub. And narrated to us Ya'qub al-Dawraqi, Ziyad ibn Ayyub Abu Hashim, and Mu'ammal ibn Hisham—they said: narrated to us Isma'il, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, that a man from Quraysh said to 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr: ""I am burdened with family and dependents. Our only means of transport are these slow donkeys. Should I depart from Muzdalifah at night?""

He (Ibn 'Amr) replied: ""As for Ibrahim (peace be upon him), he spent the night at Mina until morning, until the sun rose and its rays appeared, then he proceeded to 'Arafah until he reached his place there."" Mu'ammal said: ""His place from 'Arafah.""

They (the narrators) said: ""Then he set out and took his position there."" Mu'ammal said: ""From there."" They said: ""Until the sun set, then he departed and reached Muzdalifah.""

Ziyad said: ""He spent the night there, then when it was time for the early morning prayer, he stood until the time of the sunrise prayer, then he departed.""

Thus is the way of your father Ibrahim (peace be upon him). Your Prophet (ﷺ) has been commanded to follow him in this matter.""

This is the narration of Ibn 'Ulayyah."
